WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.140
The first film ended on a gel, with sickle hemoglobin trailing normal hemoglobin.

2
00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.460
But a slower drift in a gel cannot bend a red cell.

3
00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.780
So what does that one valine actually do?

4
00:00:16.183 --> 00:00:17.743
Start with what the gel saw.

5
00:00:19.123 --> 00:00:24.563
Glutamate's side chain is an acid switch, and at blood pH it has let go: −1.

6
00:00:26.023 --> 00:00:28.463
Valine has no switch at all, so it carries nothing.

7
00:00:29.583 --> 00:00:34.663
Hemoglobin has two beta chains, so the swap removes two negative charges per

8
00:00:34.663 --> 00:00:41.223
molecule. In an alkaline buffer, every hemoglobin is net negative and drifts toward

9
00:00:41.223 --> 00:00:42.223
the positive pole.

10
00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:47.023
Sickle hemoglobin, two charges less negative, falls behind.

11
00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:53.433
But a side chain has a second property: polarity, how comfortable it is in water.

12
00:00:54.793 --> 00:00:58.033
Glutamate is charged and hydrophilic, at ease in water.

13
00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:01.513
Valine is a greasy hydrocarbon chain.

14
00:01:02.813 --> 00:01:03.933
It is hydrophobic.

15
00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:10.193
In a protein that dissolves in water, greasy side chains tend to be buried in the

16
00:01:10.193 --> 00:01:13.233
core. There they stay away from water.

17
00:01:14.753 --> 00:01:18.053
Charged and polar side chains tend to face the water outside.

18
00:01:19.217 --> 00:01:24.597
Position six of each beta chain sits on the surface, facing water, where glutamate

19
00:01:24.597 --> 00:01:30.157
is at home. Swap in valine, and a greasy patch now sits out in the water.

20
00:01:31.217 --> 00:01:33.917
So one swap changed two properties at once.

21
00:01:35.117 --> 00:01:36.297
Here is the whole idea.

22
00:01:37.777 --> 00:01:40.057
Charge explains how the defect was first seen.

23
00:01:41.457 --> 00:01:43.357
Polarity explains why the cells sickle.

24
00:01:44.317 --> 00:01:48.057
Now watch what happens when hemoglobin gives up its oxygen.

25
00:01:49.397 --> 00:01:55.417
Its shape shifts. A matching hydrophobic pocket opens on its surface, greasy like

26
00:01:55.417 --> 00:01:56.417
the patch.

27
00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.630
Picture two of these molecules side by side.

28
00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:02.250
One carries a greasy patch.

29
00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.070
The next has a greasy pocket.

30
00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:07.990
Neither is comfortable in water.

31
00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:10.490
What happens when they meet?

32
00:02:16.433 --> 00:02:20.613
The valine patch of one molecule sticks into the pocket on the next.

33
00:02:21.713 --> 00:02:24.873
That one's patch docks into the next pocket, and so on.

34
00:02:26.273 --> 00:02:28.773
Hemoglobin chains into long, rigid fibers.

35
00:02:29.933 --> 00:02:33.473
These rigid fibers deform the red cell into a sickle.

36
00:02:34.513 --> 00:02:36.033
That is where the disease starts.

37
00:02:37.473 --> 00:02:40.733
Hemolysis, inflammation and blocked vessels follow from it.

38
00:02:41.683 --> 00:02:47.003
So read each side chain twice: once for its charge, once for its comfort in water.

39
00:02:48.223 --> 00:02:50.663
The gel counted the lost negative charges.

40
00:02:51.883 --> 00:02:53.403
The red cell felt the new grease.
